description:
In no_ack.rs lines 249-254, unsafe is used to return a reference to storage:

```rust
// SAFETY: We need to return a reference with lifetime 'a.
// The storage was borrowed with lifetime 'a, so this is safe.
let packet = unsafe {
    core::slice::from_raw_parts(self.storage.as_ptr(), self.received_len)
};
```

This works around the borrow checker but isn't necessary. The actual issue is that `receive(&mut self, ...)` takes &mut self while trying to return a reference that outlives the call.

What goes wrong: Unsafe blocks require extra scrutiny. This pattern could mask actual soundness issues in future refactoring.

Alternative 1 - Change API to return length only:
```rust
pub fn receive(&mut self, fragment: &Fragment<M, N, T>) -> NoAckResult {
    // ... same logic ...
    NoAckResult {
        complete: true,
        packet_len: Some(self.received_len),  // caller reads self.packet() 
        rcs_ok: Some(rcs_ok),
    }
}

pub fn packet(&self) -> Option<&[u8]> {
    // Only valid after complete
    Some(&self.storage[..self.received_len])
}
```

Alternative 2 - Return a separate result type that borrows storage.

